! ============================================================================
!  FROTZSMITH DEMO  —  Standard Library two-room world
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  A minimal but complete Inform 6 game using the Standard Library. It shows:
!    • Two connected rooms with a directional link (n_to / s_to)
!    • A takeable object placed inside a room with arrow (→) notation
!    • The Initialise routine setting a start location and printing a banner
!    • The three Standard Library includes (Parser / VerbLib / Grammar)
!  Try: look, take lamp, examine lamp, north, south, inventory
!
!  Lines starting with `!` are comments; the compiler ignores everything from
!  `!` to the end of the line. Read the file top to bottom: each piece of code
!  is explained just above it.
!
!  HOW THE GAME FLOWS
!    You start in the Cottage, where a brass lamp lies. TAKE LAMP (and EXAMINE
!    it), go NORTH to the Meadow and SOUTH back; INVENTORY shows the lamp.
!    No puzzle and no ending: a map with one thing in it.
!
!  MAP
!        Meadow
!          |  (north from the Cottage / south from the Meadow)
!        Cottage   <- start; the brass lamp is here
!
!  WHAT THE LIBRARY DOES FOR YOU (no code for any of it below)
!    TAKE / DROP / INVENTORY / EXAMINE / LOOK / NORTH / SOUTH all work with no
!    code of your own: the Standard Library supplies the verbs, the replies
!    ("Taken.", "You already have the brass lamp.", "You can't go that way.")
!    and the bookkeeping (where each object is). Your job is to describe the
!    world; the library makes it playable.
! ============================================================================

! --- Game identity constants -------------------------------------------------
!  `Constant` gives a name to a value fixed at compile time (it can never
!  change while the game runs).
!  `Constant Story` sets the game title, printed in bold in the opening banner.
!  `Constant Headline` sets the subtitle printed under it in the banner.
!  `^` inside a string literal is a newline character.

Constant Story "FROTZSMITH DEMO";
Constant Headline "^A tiny demo world for testing the frotzsmith IDE.^";

! --- Standard Library includes (Parser must come first) ---------------------
!  `Include "file";` reads another source file at this exact point, as if its
!  text were pasted here. The library is three such files.
!  Include "Parser" pulls in the parsing engine. It reads the player's input
!  line, tokenises it, and turns it into an action + noun(s). It also defines
!  the property and attribute names (description, n_to, light ...) used below.

Include "Parser";

!  Include "VerbLib" adds handlers for all built-in verbs: Take, Drop,
!  Examine, Look, Go, Inventory, Put, Open, Close, etc. Your own rooms and
!  objects should be defined AFTER VerbLib and BEFORE Grammar.

Include "VerbLib";

! --- Initialise routine -----------------------------------------------------
!  A routine is code between square brackets: `[ Name; statements ];`.
!  The library calls [ Initialise; ] once before the first prompt (it is an
!  "entry point": the library looks for a routine with exactly this name).
!  `location = Cottage` sets the player's starting room. In the Standard
!  Library this global variable MUST be assigned here;
!  PunyInform uses INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE instead.
!
!  `print "…^^"` prints the string; `^` is a newline so `^^` gives a blank
!  line. Two separate print statements are used here so each message can be
!  edited independently.
!
!  The routine ends at `];` without a return statement, so it returns false
!  (0). That is fine: only a return value of 2 would change anything (it
!  tells the library to skip the banner). The welcome text therefore appears
!  first, then the banner, then the first LOOK of the Cottage.

[ Initialise;
    location = Cottage;
    print "^Welcome to the frotzsmith demo!^^";
    print "Try: look, take lamp, examine lamp, north, south, inventory.^";
];

! --- Room: Cottage ----------------------------------------------------------
!  `Object Cottage "Cottage"` declares a room object. The identifier `Cottage`
!  is used in code (e.g., `location = Cottage`; `n_to Cottage`). The quoted
!  string "Cottage" is the short name shown to the player.
!
!  Inform has no separate "room" keyword: a room is simply an object that
!  nothing else contains, which the player can be inside.
!
!  After the header come two lists:
!    with ... — PROPERTIES: named values, separated by commas.
!    has  ... — ATTRIBUTES: on/off flags, separated by spaces.
!  The final `;` ends the whole object.
!
!  `with description "…"` — the long description printed on >LOOK and each
!  time the player walks in (the 6.12 library starts in VERBOSE mode, so the
!  description is repeated on every visit; BRIEF changes that). Any `^`
!  characters inside produce newlines.
!
!  `n_to Meadow` — the n_to property tells the parser that going NORTH from
!  this room moves the player to the Meadow object. Other direction properties:
!  s_to, e_to, w_to, ne_to, nw_to, se_to, sw_to, u_to, d_to, in_to, out_to.
!  A direction the room has no property for gets "You can't go that way."
!  Note that `Meadow` is used here before it is declared further down: the
!  compiler allows an object to be named before its own declaration.
!
!  The "sturdy door" in the text is only words. It is not an object, so
!  EXAMINE DOOR answers "You can't see any such thing." Anything the player
!  is invited to look at should be given an object (see the `scenery`
!  attribute in later samples).
!
!  `has light` — marks the room as lit. Without this attribute the room is
!  completely dark and the player cannot interact with anything inside it.

Object Cottage "Cottage"
  with description
        "A cosy one-room cottage. A sturdy door leads north into daylight.",
       n_to Meadow,
  has  light;

! --- Object: lamp (inside Cottage) ------------------------------------------
!  `Object ->` (arrow notation) places this object inside the most recently
!  defined room or container at compile time. One arrow = one level deep.
!  So the lamp starts the game on the floor of the Cottage. (Without the
!  arrow the lamp would be "nowhere": it would exist, but no room would
!  contain it, so the player could never find it.)
!
!  `with name 'brass' 'lamp'` — the `name` property lists the dictionary words
!  the parser accepts when the player refers to this object. Words are in
!  single quotes. The parser matches noun phrases against these words, so
!  "take brass lamp", "get lamp", "take brass" all work.
!  Single quotes = dictionary words (what the player may TYPE); double
!  quotes = text the game PRINTS. The short name "brass lamp" is printed,
!  but the parser never reads it: only `name` words are understood.
!
!  `description "…"` — the text printed in response to >EXAMINE LAMP.
!  Objects without a description property get a default "You see nothing
!  special about …" message from the library.
!
!  Note: no `has` line is needed for a basic portable object. The library
!  allows taking any object that doesn't have the `static` or `scenery`
!  attribute. Adding `has light` to the lamp would make it a working light
!  source that illuminates dark rooms when carried (see The Dark Cellar).
!
!  The room listing "You can see a brass lamp here." is written by the
!  library from the short name; the article "a" is added automatically.

Object -> lamp "brass lamp"
  with name 'brass' 'lamp',
       description "A small brass lamp, the kind every adventurer carries.";

! --- Room: Meadow -----------------------------------------------------------
!  A second room connected to the Cottage via s_to (going SOUTH returns the
!  player to the Cottage). Exits are one-way in Inform: the Cottage's n_to
!  does not create this s_to; each room lists its own ways out. Note that
!  Meadow also has `has light`; every room the player can reach should have
!  a light source (the `light` attribute on the room itself, or a carried
!  lamp) or it will be dark.
!
!  Meadow is declared without an arrow, so it is a top-level object (a room),
!  not something inside the lamp.

Object Meadow "Meadow"
  with description
        "A sunlit meadow dotted with wildflowers. The cottage lies to the south.",
       s_to Cottage,
  has  light;

! --- Grammar (must be the final include) ------------------------------------
!  Include "Grammar" comes last. It builds the grammar tables that map verb
!  words ('take', 'get', 'north', 'i' ...) to the actions VerbLib defines,
!  and the library refuses to compile it before VerbLib. New verbs of your
!  own (`Verb`) can be declared before or after it; `Extend`, which adds to
!  one of the library's verbs, has to come after it (see Custom Grammar).
!
!  Include "Exits" is a small frotzsmith helper (not the official library):
!  after every room description it prints "Obvious exits: north." by
!  checking which direction properties the room has.

Include "Exits";    ! frotzsmith: auto-list obvious exits (see Exits.h)
Include "Grammar";
